[Music]  google compute engine enables you to  deploy updates to virtual machine  instances in managed instance groups  using two methods  namely  rolling update incrementally deploys  updates in all vms of the mig  canary deployment it is a deployment  strategy that releases an application or  service incrementally to a subset of  instances in mig  in a prior video we learned how to  perform rolling update to a mig in this  video we will learn how to do a cannery  update for migs here are a few benefits  of canary update it gives you the  flexibility to roll out new changes to a  subset of vms and assess impact and user  experience  can redeployments can be rolled back  quickly if something goes wrong during  an update then rolling back a subset of  vms is faster than rolling back all the  vms this can help minimize the  disruption to your end users it is less  expensive than rolling updates because  it uses less infrastructure there are  also some drawbacks to using canva  deployments they can be more time  consuming and error prone if they don't  use automation  implementation can be tricky for some  specific type of database applications  similar to rolling update a canary  deployment also support two type of  update modes automatic or proactive  opportunistic or selective  to learn more about this follow the link  given in the description  now let's look at how to perform can we  update in proactive mode in the google  cloud console go to compute engine  instance groups  in our project we have our instance  group can reupdate proactive mode this  mig has five instances  we have  two instance templates which we use to  create and update our migs  application current version the current  instance template and application new  version the new instance template that  we will update to select the mig that  you want to update in this example we  will select canary update proactive mode  choose update vms  under new template select the new  template to update to  we will select application new version  under target size enter the percentage  or fixed number of instances you want to  use to carry the new instance template  for this example we will specify one  instance this means that 20 percent of  the instances will be updated while the  other instances will remain on the  current version under update  configuration select the update type to  be automatic click on update vms to  start the canary update to monitor the  progress of the canary update open the  mig in a separate window  notice that only one instance is running  with the new version while the remaining  instance continued to run with the  current version  congratulations you just learned how to  perform canary update in proactive mode  if you need to do a quick rollback of  the canva update you can do so using  google cloud cli or the rest api  for canary update the best practice is  to fully roll back and update before you  try to update to another version let's  dive into a quick rollback demo using  the google cloud cli  run this command with the name of your  mig and the version you want to roll  back  in this command  canary update proactive mode is the name  of our mig  application new version is the name of  the configuration template we want to  roll back to  once the command completes let's go to  instance group page  congratulations you have successfully  rolled back the instances to its  previous version for more information  about cannery updates for mig check out  the documentation  thanks for watching  [Music]  you

